// Where a line may be broken — UAX #14.
//
// Wrapping on spaces is wrong in three directions at once. It breaks
// between a number and its unit, between an opening bracket and what it
// opens, and before a full stop; it refuses to break Chinese and Japanese
// at all, which have no spaces and break almost anywhere; and it breaks
// inside `10 000` and `1,000`, which are one number.
//
// UAX #14 answers the question properly: given a string, which positions
// *may* a renderer break at. It does not say which one to choose — that is
// the layout engine's job and depends on the width — and it does not
// hyphenate.
//
// ## Three kinds of rule
//
//   - **Prohibitions** that reach backwards past spaces: an opening
//     bracket forbids a break after it *and* after the spaces that follow
//     it, so `( a` cannot break in either place.
//   - **Pairs**, which are most of it: a class on the left and a class on
//     the right decide.
//   - **Runs**, for numbers: `(PR|PO)? (OP|HY)? NU (NU|SY|IS)* (CL|CP)?
//     (PO|PR)?` is one unit, which is what keeps `$1,234.00.` together and
//     lets the sentence's full stop go to the next line.
//
// ## The default is to break
//
// Anything the rules do not join may be broken between, which is why the
// class table has to be complete: an unassigned code point is treated as
// `AL`, a letter, rather than as a place to break.

/// Four numbers per run: the first code point, the last, the
/// Line_Break class after the substitutions rule LB1 asks for, and
/// a set of flags the later rules need: 1 an initial quote, 2 a
/// final quote, 4 East Asian wide, fullwidth or halfwidth, 8 an
/// unassigned pictograph, 16 the dotted circle the Brahmic rules
/// name.
